{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t09-008",
  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "Kubernetes workloads configuration scheduling and networking",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Kubernetes stores desired API objects and controllers continuously reconcile actual state toward that desired state.",
    "A Pod is the smallest schedulable unit and groups containers that share network identity and selected storage. Workload controllers create and replace Pods; directly created Pods have no higher-level controller to recreate them.",
    "Deployments manage interchangeable stateless replicas and rollout history. StatefulSets add stable identities and ordered behavior; DaemonSets target nodes; Jobs and CronJobs represent finite work.",
    "A Service selects a stable set of endpoints while Pods change. Ingress is the established HTTP routing API but is frozen; Kubernetes recommends Gateway API for new capabilities. Neither API works without a compatible controller or implementation.",
    "Configuration, resource requests, probes, security context, and scheduling constraints influence whether a Pod can start safely and receive traffic. Liveness restarts, readiness controls traffic eligibility, and startup delays the other probes.",
    "Kubernetes provides APIs, not every implementation: storage classes, network policies, ingress controllers, metrics adapters, and cloud load balancers depend on cluster components and providers."
  ],
